<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Trigly mark on a neutral plate. This is `<application android:icon>`, and
  it is the one icon in this app that cannot follow the chosen colour scheme.

  WHY IT CANNOT FOLLOW. From Android 12 the platform draws an icon inside every
  toast, and SystemUI picks that icon itself: it reads the package's
  `ApplicationInfo` (`getApplicationInfoAsUser`) and loads
  `IconDrawableFactory.getBadgedIcon(appInfo, user)`. That is the
  `<application>` icon. It is never an `activity-alias` icon, and the nine
  aliases are the whole mechanism by which the launcher icon follows the
  scheme, so none of them is reachable from the toast. `PackageManager` offers
  no way to change an application icon at run time either: it has
  `getApplicationIcon` and no setter. See `ToastAction` for the full finding.

  The same icon is what the launcher badges a pinned rule shortcut with, and
  what Settings and the share sheet show.

  WHY A PLATE, AFTER TWO ATTEMPTS WITHOUT ONE. The mark shipped plate-less in
  0.3.2 and it was wrong twice over, both times because a bare mark has to be
  legible on a ground nobody here chooses.

  First it was one colour for both toast backgrounds, the brand orange, which
  cleared the 3:1 floor on each and no more. Then it was ink in light and
  white in dark, a `values-night` pair, which reads far better on those two
  grounds and fails on a third: the launcher's shortcut badge sits on a white
  plate in EITHER theme, and a night qualifier cannot tell that plate from a
  dark toast, so the white half arrived as a white mark on a white badge.

  A plate ends the argument rather than winning it. With an opaque background
  the mark is legible against its own plate and nothing else, so no ground the
  platform picks can take it away, and the contrast is a number this repo
  controls: ink on `Tone.Neutral90` is about 14.7:1.

  WHY NOT THE ORANGE PLATE THE LAUNCHER ICON WEARS. `ic_launcher.xml` and its
  eight preset siblings each carry the colour of the scheme they belong to, and
  this icon cannot change with the scheme. It would therefore be one fixed
  orange sitting beside eight other colours, claiming to be the scheme's icon
  and being wrong for eight of the nine. A neutral plate makes no claim about
  the scheme at all, which is the honest thing for an icon that cannot follow
  one.

  ADAPTIVE AGAIN, WHICH THE PLATE-LESS VERSION COULD NOT BE. `IconDrawableFactory`
  sends an adaptive drawable through `LauncherIcons.wrapIconDrawableWithShadow`,
  which draws a blurred shadow of the icon *mask*. That is the correct look for
  an icon with a plate, and it is what every other app icon gets. It was only
  a fault while the background was transparent, when the shadow was all that
  was left of it. Being adaptive also means a launcher's mask fits this icon
  the way it fits every other, and the foreground's own 0.94 keeps the bar of
  the T clear of a circular mask.

  The foreground is `ic_launcher_foreground.xml` itself, not a copy. The mark
  is the same mark at the same size; only the plate behind it differs. A copy
  would be one more file to forget the day the mark changes.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_app_mark_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <!--
      Themed icons tint the alpha of this layer to the wallpaper palette, the
      same as `ic_launcher.xml` does, so the mark still reads where a launcher
      themes it.
    -->
    <monochrome android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_foreground" />
</adaptive-icon>
